PUBLIC RESPONSE: The presentation (systolic ejection murmur maximal at the LUSB that intensifies with inspiration + thrill) indicates turbulent flow across the pulmonic valve. The ECG combination of RAD-equivalent RVH plus left-axis deviation is classically produced by an ostium-primum ASD (abnormal left-anterior fascicle + right-ventricular volume overload). The childhood “faint cyanosis” is consistent with transient right-to-left shunting across the ASD before pulmonary pressures rose. No other listed lesion simultaneously explains the location/timing of the murmur, its respiratory behavior, and the distinctive ECG axis. Therefore the murmur is caused by an atrial septal defect (choice D).

(The patient’s ethnicity is likely irrelevant and serves only as a distractor.)

}

